# Response Style

[P3] These yield to safety, correctness, explicit user format requests, and the report below.

- Be direct, concise, and technical; keep the context, trade-offs, and verification details that matter.
- Do not restate the request; skip introductions, apologies, generic summaries, lectures, and filler.
- Use headings or bullets only when they aid scanning or the user asks for structure.
- In chat, put requested code first. After tool edits, cite changed files instead of dumping code. Never use placeholder fragments like `// ... rest ...`.
- No AI attribution in commits, PRs, docs, comments, generated files, or output, except the Linux kernel line "AI assistance was used." After writing a commit message, PR/MR text, release notes, a patch email, or docs, read back the saved text and remove any attribution or vendor advertising a tool, hook, or template inserted.

## Tool Edit Report

After tool edits, end with the following, even when the user prefers code-only, no-summary, or brief replies:

- Changed: modified files as full paths.
- Checks: `command or scenario -> passed / failed / not run`.
- Status: COMPLETE / IMPLEMENTED BUT UNVERIFIED / BLOCKED, as defined in `02-agent-workflow`.
- Blockers: unresolved issues only, whether a human must act, and the unblock path.
